\title{A guided reading of the crossing-number proof}
\taxon{Overview}
\meta{dependencies}{None.}
\meta{provenance}{Expository organization and source review, 9 October 2026.}

\p{The paper seeks an exact [crossing count](cn-0005), not merely an asymptotic estimate. Its [balanced-axis construction](cn-0036) gives the familiar upper bound. The central task is a matching lower bound for every admissible drawing, including curved edges and repeated crossings.}

\transclude{cn-0007}

\p{\strong{Three branches meet at one inequality.} Start with [the rank-sum corollary](cn-0027): matrices #{X_i,Y_j} with invertible diagonal differences force a large total off-diagonal rank. The [algebraic branch](cn-0061) proves this using polynomial constraints on subspaces and determinant multiplicity.}

\p{Next read [the topological branch](cn-0060). Correcting signed crossings by half-order terms at endpoints makes cycle pairings vanish. Four-cycles convert this into a block identity modulo row-plus-column matrices. This is the source of the difference form #{R^{ik}=X_i-Y_k}.}

\p{Finally read [the detector branch](cn-0062). Its linear map kills row-plus-column terms and same-second-endpoint terms; a single nonzero crossing entry contributes at most one rank. Yet every diagonal star-order block has full rank #{s^2}. The [worked example](cn-0040) shows an explicit image and its determinant.}

\p{With these three ingredients understood, the entire odd-size lower bound is the following short assembly.}

\transclude{cn-0035}

\p{There is a reason to prove odd second-part sizes first: the detector has dimension #{s^2=d_{2s+1}} and uses #{2s+1} nodes. Even sizes are obtained by a count over vertex deletions.}

\transclude{cn-0037}
\transclude{cn-0036}

\p{\strong{How Lean helps.} [The Lean route](cn-0038) exposes exactly these interfaces: the central module proves the difference form, checks diagonal invertibility, obtains the rank lower bound, and bounds ranks by actual crossing points. Its many geometric support files expand the normalization and shifted-track arguments. The [source and check record](cn-0039) states what was inspected and what was not run locally.}

\p{\strong{Bibliographic context.} The introduction connects rotations to earlier work by [Kleitman](cn-0052) and [Woodall](cn-0057), then to [de Klerk et al.](cn-0046), [de Klerk–Pasechnik–Schrijver](cn-0047), and [Brosch–Polak](cn-0045). It also discusses [the flag-algebra lower bound](cn-0043), based on [Razborov's method](cn-0054), and [Tutte's algebraic viewpoint](cn-0056). These supply context; the three proof branches above contain the mathematical inputs of the new argument. The [references tree](cn-0041) includes the remaining historical sources.}
